      "claim": "Batimastat, a poorly soluble peptidomimetic with amide/hydroxamate functionality, showed low/poor oral bioavailability in patients (C05, C06, C08, C27), with L2 records describing it as nonorally bioavailable (C32) and poor (C36); this supports low F for the similarly peptide-ester-like, poorly soluble query.",
      "card_ids": ["C05", "C06", "C08", "C27", "C32", "C36"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Felodipine, a lipophilic neutral drug with low solubility and extensive first-pass metabolism, had absolute oral F of 15.4% (solution) and 12.5% (tablet) at 10 mg fasted (C01, C04), 13.2% at 5 mg (C11), 12.1% in hypertensive patients (C25), and 17.0–17.1% in cirrhosis (C19, C22, C29), with L2 records confirming 17% unchanged in cirrhosis (C33, C40); all below 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C04", "C11", "C25", "C19", "C22", "C29", "C33", "C40"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Nimodipine, another lipophilic neutral poorly soluble dihydropyridine, had absolute oral F of 3.5–3.8% across 30, 60, and 90 mg fasted doses (C03, C09, C21, C28), below 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C03", "C09", "C21", "C28"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 records for testosterone undecanoate show that lipid vehicles/food can improve its bioavailability relative to crystalline drug (C37, C38), but the accepted absolute F remains 6.8% (C15, C30); this is a formulation caveat, not affirmative evidence that the query reaches 20%.",
